# Line endings.
#
# `core.autocrlf` already normalises to LF *in the index*, so what is pushed is
# always correct. The risk is the working copy: on Windows these files get CRLF
# on checkout, and `docker build` copies the working tree rather than the
# index — so an image built on Windows ships an entrypoint whose shebang reads
# `#!/bin/sh\r` and dies with "bad interpreter". Pinning the ones that are
# executed by a shell inside the container makes that impossible rather than
# unlikely.
*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# Everything else is normalised in the index and left to the platform in the
# working copy, which is the sane default.
* text=auto

# Binary, so git never tries.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.gif  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip  binary
*.ico  binary
